+//! Fire Emblem Fates randomizer, the full swap: names and voices in message text (round 74,
+//! docs/rando/FULL_BLOCK.md section 4, docs/rando/FULL_SWAP_PLAN.md section 2).
+//!
+//! In a run made with "How they look" = full swap, the person standing in A's spot is B: B's
+//! face, name plate and menu name come from the table cells (patch.txt). The story's lines
+//! still name A, and A's voiced lines would still play. This module changes the message
+//! text the game fetches, in guest memory only: no message or script file is ever written.
+//!
+//! **Where.** `Mess::Get` (0x003D1CEC; `push {r4-r8, lr}`, the label kept in r7 from its
+//! entry) has three return paths and all of them pass 0x003D1DA8 (`add sp, sp, #80`, then
+//! `pop {r4-r8, pc}`) with the text in r0: the label-not-found path branches there from
+//! 0x003D1D18, a plain message from 0x003D1D90 (r0 = the text inside the loaded message
+//! file), a built message (arguments `$a`, or a prefix entry) falls into it from 0x003D1DA4
+//! (r0 = the game's one shared message buffer, 0x006DCBDC +4). Words read from the owner's
+//! dump. While a run whose `full.txt` has something for this game is active, the frame hook
+//! writes a reserved svc (0xEFFEC002) over that `add` and has the recompiled code checked
+//! again, so the interpreter runs `Mess::Get` (round 71's chest trap is the same kind,
+//! `fates_rando_chests`). At the svc the host does the `add` itself, reads the text at r0
+//! and, when the pass below changes it, writes the changed text into its own ring of buffers
+//! and points r0 there. Then `pop` returns to the caller as always.
+//!
+//! **The second place** (round 74's repair, found by the robot's P2 proof): story scenes and
+//! support talks do not come through `Mess::Get` but through `Mess::GetNoReplace` (0x003D16CC,
+//! `push {r4-r6, lr}`; called from `ProcTalkManager::Initialize` and
+//! `TalkExpander::ExpandMessage(const char*)`). It is the same lookup without filling in
+//! arguments and has its own return at 0x003D1784 (`add sp, sp, #80`, then `pop {r4-r6, pc}`,
+//! the same word), text in r0. Its label is not kept in a register (r5 is reloaded at
+//! 0x003D1720), but both functions print `<label>_COM` into their stack buffer at sp (the
+//! prefix entry's label, format at 0x00644CF8), so the trap reads the label there. The same
+//! svc number is written at both places; `on_svc` tells them apart by the address.
+//!
+//! **Built messages are left** (the same repair): when `Mess::Get` fills in arguments (the
+//! text at r4 starts with `$a`, the result is the shared buffer), the arguments are names the
+//! game took through the persons' own labels, which already name the filler; passing the built
+//! text again would chain B -> C ("Kaze and Rinkah attained support level B." where keep said
+//! "Kaze and Hana"). Such a text is returned as the game built it.
+//!
+//! **Repeats share one copy.** The talk manager keeps its scene text for the whole scene and a
+//! menu may fetch the same text every frame, so a changed text that is already in the younger
+//! half of the ring (same bytes) is handed out again instead of written again.
+//!
+//! **Why a ring.** Callers keep two results at once (round 73b: 36 places, for example
+//! `ProcFortune::Persistent` passes two texts together to `castle::cut::DrawLowerShop`), so
+//! the game's one shared buffer is not safe. The ring is one block of `RING_SIZE` bytes in
+//! guest memory; each changed text takes the next free bytes, wrapping to the start, so a
+//! result stays valid until a whole ring of later changed text has been written after it.
+//!
+//! **The region.** `RING_VADDR` (0x50000000, 1 MiB), mapped by this module the first time a
+//! full run arms the trap, with pages from the Base region of FCRAM (where the HLE services
+//! take theirs: gsp, hid, apt). Why it is free: a 3DS process is never given an address at
+//! or above 0x40000000 (zakuro_common::memory_map: code image from 0x00100000, heap
+//! 0x08000000-0x10000000, shared memory 0x10000000-0x14000000, the old linear heap
+//! 0x14000000-0x1C000000, VRAM / DSP / config / TLS 0x1F000000-0x1FFFFFFF, the new linear
+//! heap 0x30000000-0x40000000; every fixed `Memory::map` in the core lies below
+//! 0x40000000), so the game's code holds no pointer there; the page table is a flat 32-bit
+//! one, so the recompiled code and the interpreter read it like any page; and the module
+//! maps it only when `Memory::mapping_at` finds nothing over the range (else the trap stays
+//! off, with a notice). Once mapped it stays mapped for the program's life, so a pointer a
+//! caller still holds never dangles; a session that never had a full run never maps it.
+//!
+//! **The pass** (one per text, in this order):
+//! 0. A built `Mess::Get` text (arguments filled in) is left alone (above).
+//! 1. A text whose label (r7) starts with `MPID_` is left alone: person names and help texts
+//!    are fetched through the person's own labels, which the table cells already point at
+//!    the filler's (menus, name plates and the battle window then show B's own name; changing
+//!    it again would chain B -> C).
+//! 2. Commands (`$` and its name, then its arguments, see `command_end`) are copied as they
+//!    are, except a `$Svp` (voice play) command whose label starts with `VOICE_<key>_` for a
+//!    silenced key, which is dropped whole.
+//! 3. In plain text every whole-word occurrence of an old name becomes the new name, all pairs
+//!    in one pass, the longest old name first at each place; replaced text is never scanned
+//!    again (A -> B and B -> C never chain). A word letter is an ASCII letter or digit or a
+//!    code unit in U+00C0..U+024F; a command counts as a word break.
+//! 4. Nothing changes and the original pointer stays when the text has no listed name and no
+//!    silenced voice, or the chapter is on an `off` line.
+//!
+//! **Off means original.** No `full.txt` (look = keep: every run the owner's page can make,
+//! every run made before round 74, Randomizer off), a `full.txt` with nothing for this game's
+//! text language and no voice key, or one that does not parse: neither word (0x003D1DA8,
+//! 0x003D1784) is ever written and no memory is mapped. When the run ends the original word goes back at
+//! the next frame; a save state taken with the svc in memory is put right the same way.
+
+use std::collections::HashSet;
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use crate::fates_rando::{self, Guest};
+use crate::System;
+
+/// `Mess::Get`'s shared return: `add sp, sp, #80`.
+pub const MESS_GET_RETURN: u32 = 0x003D_1DA8;
+/// `Mess::GetNoReplace`'s shared return (story scenes, support talks): `add sp, sp, #80`.
+pub const NOREPLACE_RETURN: u32 = 0x003D_1784;
+/// both places the trap is written, in this order.
+pub const TRAP_SITES: [u32; 2] = [MESS_GET_RETURN, NOREPLACE_RETURN];
+/// the original word at both.
+pub const ORIGINAL_WORD: u32 = 0xE28D_D050;
+/// the end both functions print after the label in their stack buffer (`%s_COM`).
+const COM_SUFFIX: &[u8] = b"_COM";
+/// that buffer's size (`snprintf(sp, 0x50, ...)`).
+const LABEL_BUF: u32 = 0x50;
+/// changed texts remembered for handing out again.
+const RECENT: usize = 64;
+/// the bytes `add sp, sp, #80` gives back.
+pub const STACK_ADD: u32 = 80;
+/// the reserved svc number (round 71's chest trap has 0xFEC001; a later trap takes 0xFEC003).
+pub const SVC_NUMBER: u32 = 0x00FE_C002;
+/// `svc #0xFEC002`
+pub const TRAP_WORD: u32 = 0xEF00_0000 | SVC_NUMBER;
+/// the file in the run folder (`rando.py make --patch-out` writes it beside patch.txt; the
+/// core keeps it with the run, `fates_rando_session::make_run_inner`).
+pub const FILE: &str = "full.txt";
+pub const FORMAT: u32 = 1;
+const MAGIC: &str = "fates-rando-full";
+/// the text language this game shows (`m/@E/`: the only one in Fates USA and Seal of Flames).
+pub const TEXT_LANG: &str = "E";
+/// the ring of buffers: where and how big.
+pub const RING_VADDR: u32 = 0x5000_0000;
+pub const RING_SIZE: u32 = 0x0010_0000;
+/// the longest text the pass reads, in UTF-16 code units (the longest English message is
+/// under 5,000); a longer or unterminated text is left as it is.
+pub const MAX_TEXT_UNITS: usize = 0x4000;
+/// the longest label read from r7.
+const MAX_LABEL: u32 = 64;
+/// the longest name and the longest command argument the pass looks at.
+const MAX_NAME_UNITS: usize = 64;
+const MAX_ARG_UNITS: usize = 64;
+/// labels said in the log once each, at most this many.
+const MAX_LOGGED: usize = 2000;
+
+const DOLLAR: u16 = b'$' as u16;
+const BAR: u16 = b'|' as u16;
+const NEWLINE: u16 = b'\n' as u16;
+
+// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the file
+
+/// one name pair: in text language `lang`, `old` (A's display name) becomes `new` (B's), both
+/// as UTF-16 code units.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Name {
+    pub lang: String,
+    pub old: Vec<u16>,
+    pub new: Vec<u16>,
+}
+
+/// a run's `full.txt`.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Full {
+    pub seed: String,
+    pub game: String,
+    pub names: Vec<Name>,
+    /// voice keys (ASCII) whose `$Svp VOICE_<key>_...` commands are dropped
+    pub silence: Vec<Vec<u8>>,
+    /// chapters in which nothing is changed
+    pub off: Vec<String>,
+}
+
+fn unhex(s: &str) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
+    if s.len() % 2 != 0 || s.is_empty() {
+        return None;
+    }
+    (0..s.len()).step_by(2).map(|i| s.get(i..i + 2).and_then(|b| u8::from_str_radix(b, 16).ok())).collect()
+}
+
+fn units(b: &[u8]) -> Option<Vec<u16>> {
+    if b.len() % 2 != 0 {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let u: Vec<u16> = b.chunks(2).map(|c| u16::from_le_bytes([c[0], c[1]])).collect();
+    (!u.is_empty() && u.len() <= MAX_NAME_UNITS && !u.contains(&0)).then_some(u)
+}
+
+fn key_ok(k: &[u8]) -> bool {
+    !k.is_empty() && k.len() <= 32 && k.iter().all(|&b| b.is_ascii_graphic() && b != b'|' && b != b'$')
+}
+
+fn lang_ok(l: &str) -> bool {
+    !l.is_empty() && l.len() <= 8 && l.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || b == b'_' || b == b'-')
+}
+
+impl Full {
+    /// reads a `full.txt` (FULL_BLOCK.md section 4). A line it does not know, a missing or
+    /// wrong end line (a file not written whole), a name pair whose old and new are the same
+    /// or an old name listed twice for one language refuses the whole file. Line order is
+    /// not trusted: the pass sorts for itself.
+    pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<Full, String> {
+        let mut lines = text.lines().map(str::trim).filter(|l| !l.is_empty());
+        if lines.next() != Some(&format!("{MAGIC} {FORMAT}")[..]) {
+            return Err(format!("not a full swap file of format {FORMAT}"));
+        }
+        let mut f = Full::default();
+        let (mut count, mut end) = (0usize, None);
+        for line in lines {
+            if end.is_some() {
+                return Err("a line after end".into());
+            }
+            let bad = || format!("bad line {line:?}");
+            let parts: Vec<&str> = line.split_whitespace().collect();
+            match parts.as_slice() {
+                ["seed", s] if f.seed.is_empty() => f.seed = s.to_string(),
+                ["game", g] if f.game.is_empty() => f.game = g.to_string(),
+                ["n", lang, old, new] => {
+                    let (Some(old), Some(new)) = (unhex(old).and_then(|b| units(&b)), unhex(new).and_then(|b| units(&b)))
+                    else {
+                        return Err(bad());
+                    };
+                    if !lang_ok(lang) || old == new {
+                        return Err(bad());
+                    }
+                    if f.names.iter().any(|n| n.lang == *lang && n.old == old) {
+                        return Err(format!("an old name listed twice ({line:?})"));
+                    }
+                    f.names.push(Name { lang: lang.to_string(), old, new });
+                    count += 1;
+                }
+                ["s", key] => {
+                    let key = unhex(key).filter(|k| key_ok(k)).ok_or_else(bad)?;
+                    if !f.silence.contains(&key) {
+                        f.silence.push(key);
+                    }
+                    count += 1;
+                }
+                ["off", ch] => {
+                    let ch = crate::fates_rando_chests::chapter_key(ch).filter(|k| k == ch).ok_or_else(bad)?;
+                    if !f.off.contains(&ch) {
+                        f.off.push(ch);
+                    }
+                    count += 1;
+                }
+                ["end", n] => {
+                    let n: usize = n.parse().map_err(|_| bad())?;
+                    if n != count {
+                        return Err(format!("end says {n}, the file has {count}"));
+                    }
+                    end = Some(n);
+                }
+                _ => return Err(bad()),
+            }
+        }
+        if end.is_none() {
+            return Err("no end line (the file is not complete)".into());
+        }
+        if f.seed.is_empty() || f.game.is_empty() {
+            return Err("no seed or game line".into());
+        }
+        Ok(f)
+    }
+}
+
+/// the run folder's `full.txt`: None = no file (look = keep: the trap stays off).
+pub fn read_full(run_dir: &Path) -> Option<Result<Full, String>> {
+    match std::fs::read_to_string(run_dir.join(FILE)) {
+        Ok(text) => Some(Full::parse(&text)),
+        Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound => None,
+        Err(e) => Some(Err(e.to_string())),
+    }
+}
+
+// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the pass
+
+/// what the pass uses: one language's names, longest first, and the voice prefixes.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Swap {
+    names: Vec<(Vec<u16>, Vec<u16>)>,
+    /// `VOICE_<key>_` as code units
+    voices: Vec<Vec<u16>>,
+    off: Vec<String>,
+    firsts: HashSet<u16>,
+}
+
+impl Swap {
+    /// the pass for text language `lang`; None when it would change nothing (the fast path:
+    /// the trap is then never armed).
+    pub fn new(full: &Full, lang: &str) -> Option<Swap> {
+        let mut names: Vec<(Vec<u16>, Vec<u16>)> =
+            full.names.iter().filter(|n| n.lang == lang).map(|n| (n.old.clone(), n.new.clone())).collect();
+        names.sort_by(|a, b| b.0.len().cmp(&a.0.len()).then_with(|| a.0.cmp(&b.0)));
+        let voices: Vec<Vec<u16>> = full.silence.iter()
+            .map(|k| b"VOICE_".iter().chain(k.iter()).chain(b"_".iter()).map(|&b| b as u16).collect())
+            .collect();
+        if names.is_empty() && voices.is_empty() {
+            return None;
+        }
+        let firsts = names.iter().map(|n| n.0[0]).collect();
+        Some(Swap { names, voices, off: full.off.clone(), firsts })
+    }
+
+    /// whether chapter `ch` is on an `off` line.
+    pub fn off_in(&self, ch: Option<&str>) -> bool {
+        ch.is_some_and(|c| self.off.iter().any(|o| o == c))
+    }
+
+    pub fn has_off(&self) -> bool {
+        !self.off.is_empty()
+    }
+
+    /// a `$Svp` command (`cmd` = from its `$` through its `|`) of a silenced voice.
+    fn drops(&self, cmd: &[u16]) -> bool {
+        starts(cmd, b"$Svp") && self.voices.iter().any(|v| cmd[4..].starts_with(v))
+    }
+
+    /// the pass over one text (code units, no end mark): Some(changed text), or None when
+    /// nothing changes. `(names replaced, voice commands dropped)` are counted into `stats`.
+    pub fn apply(&self, text: &[u16], stats: &mut (u32, u32)) -> Option<Vec<u16>> {
+        let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(text.len() + 16);
+        let (mut i, mut prev_word, mut changed) = (0usize, false, false);
+        while i < text.len() {
+            let u = text[i];
+            if u == DOLLAR {
+                let end = command_end(text, i);
+                if self.drops(&text[i..end]) {
+                    changed = true;
+                    stats.1 += 1;
+                } else {
+                    out.extend_from_slice(&text[i..end]);
+                }
+                i = end;
+                prev_word = false;
+                continue;
+            }
+            if !prev_word && self.firsts.contains(&u) {
+                let hit = self.names.iter().find(|(old, _)| {
+                    text[i..].starts_with(old) && !text.get(i + old.len()).is_some_and(|&n| word(n))
+                });
+                if let Some((old, new)) = hit {
+                    out.extend_from_slice(new);
+                    i += old.len();
+                    prev_word = true;
+                    changed = true;
+                    stats.0 += 1;
+                    continue;
+                }
+            }
+            out.push(u);
+            prev_word = word(u);
+            i += 1;
+        }
+        changed.then_some(out)
+    }
+}
+
+/// a word letter for the whole-word rule: an ASCII letter or digit, or U+00C0..U+024F.
+pub fn word(u: u16) -> bool {
+    (u < 0x80 && (u as u8).is_ascii_alphanumeric()) || (0x00C0..=0x024F).contains(&u)
+}
+
+fn starts(t: &[u16], s: &[u8]) -> bool {
+    t.len() >= s.len() && t.iter().zip(s).all(|(&a, &b)| a == b as u16)
+}
+
+/// The message commands this game's text uses: the name after `$` and its arguments, `p` =
+/// one argument up to and with its `|`, `d` = digits with no `|`. Read from the English
+/// message files of the owner's game by counting (round 74; every command but a handful of
+/// one-off ones is here). Longest names first where one starts another.
+const COMMANDS: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
+    ("Svp", "p"), ("Ssp", "p"), ("Sbv", "pp"), ("Sbp", "pp"), ("Sbs", "p"), ("Sre", "p"),
+    ("Sls", "pp"), ("Slp", "pp"), ("Slv", "pp"), ("Srp", "pp"),
+    ("Ws", "p"), ("Wm", "pd"), ("Wa", ""), ("Wd", ""), ("Wc", ""), ("Wv", ""),
+    ("Nu", ""), ("Fi", "p"), ("Fo", "p"), ("Fw", "p"), ("VN", "p"), ("VF", "p"),
+    ("E", "p"), ("G", "p"),
+    ("k", ""), ("p", ""), ("w", "p"), ("t", "d"), ("a", ""), ("b", "p"), ("l", "p"), ("c", "p"),
+];
+
+/// where the command starting at `text[i]` (a `$`) ends (the index after it). A known command
+/// takes its arguments; an argument with no `|` within `MAX_ARG_UNITS` (before a `$` or a
+/// line break) ends the command there. An unknown command runs to the first `|` before the
+/// next `$` or line break when there is one near, else over its letters only.
+pub fn command_end(text: &[u16], i: usize) -> usize {
+    let mut j = i + 1;
+    let found = COMMANDS.iter().filter(|(n, _)| starts(&text[j..], n.as_bytes())).max_by_key(|(n, _)| n.len());
+    let Some((name, args)) = found else {
+        let bar = text[j..].iter().take(MAX_ARG_UNITS).position(|&u| u == BAR || u == DOLLAR || u == NEWLINE);
+        if let Some(k) = bar.filter(|&k| text[j + k] == BAR) {
+            return j + k + 1;
+        }
+        while j < text.len() && text[j] < 0x80 && (text[j] as u8).is_ascii_alphabetic() {
+            j += 1;
+        }
+        return j;
+    };
+    j += name.len();
+    for a in args.bytes() {
+        if a == b'p' {
+            let stop = text[j..].iter().take(MAX_ARG_UNITS + 1).position(|&u| u == BAR || u == DOLLAR || u == NEWLINE);
+            match stop {
+                Some(k) if text[j + k] == BAR => j += k + 1,
+                _ => return j,
+            }
+        } else {
+            while j < text.len() && (b'0' as u16..=b'9' as u16).contains(&text[j]) {
+                j += 1;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    j
+}
+
+// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- guest memory
+
+/// the ring of buffers in guest memory: `base`..`base + size`, the next free byte at `next`.
+/// `total` counts every byte the ring has moved on (with the bytes skipped at a wrap), so a
+/// text placed at `total` = t is whole while `total` - t <= `size`.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Ring {
+    pub base: u32,
+    pub size: u32,
+    pub next: u32,
+    pub total: u64,
+    /// recent changed texts: (hash of the bytes, where, how many bytes, `total` when placed)
+    recent: Vec<(u64, u32, u32, u64)>,
+}
+
+fn fnv(bytes: &[u8]) -> u64 {
+    bytes.iter().fold(0xCBF2_9CE4_8422_2325u64, |h, &b| (h ^ b as u64).wrapping_mul(0x0100_0000_01B3))
+}
+
+impl Ring {
+    pub fn new(base: u32, size: u32) -> Ring {
+        Ring { base, size, next: 0, total: 0, recent: Vec::new() }
+    }
+
+    /// where `len` bytes go (4-aligned), wrapping to the start when they do not fit before
+    /// the end; None for a text over a quarter of the ring (left unchanged).
+    pub fn place(&mut self, len: u32) -> Option<u32> {
+        self.place_at(len).map(|(at, _)| at)
+    }
+
+    /// as `place`, with `total` at the place.
+    fn place_at(&mut self, len: u32) -> Option<(u32, u64)> {
+        let need = len.checked_add(3)? & !3;
+        if need == 0 || need > self.size / 4 {
+            return None;
+        }
+        if self.next + need > self.size {
+            self.total += (self.size - self.next) as u64;
+            self.next = 0;
+        }
+        let at = self.base + self.next;
+        let start = self.total;
+        self.next += need;
+        self.total += need as u64;
+        Some((at, start))
+    }
+
+    /// a copy of `bytes` placed not long ago (in the younger half of the ring), still whole.
+    fn young(&self, hash: u64, len: u32) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> + '_ {
+        self.recent.iter().rev()
+            .filter(move |&&(h, _, l, t)| h == hash && l == len && self.total - t <= (self.size / 2) as u64)
+            .map(|&(_, at, _, _)| at)
+    }
+}
+
+/// the UTF-16 text at `addr` up to its end mark (at most `max` units), read a page piece at
+/// a time; None when unreadable or longer.
+pub fn read_text(g: &mut dyn Guest, addr: u32, max: usize) -> Option<Vec<u16>> {
+    if addr == 0 || addr % 2 != 0 {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let mut out = Vec::new();
+    let mut at = addr;
+    while out.len() < max {
+        let page_left = 0x1000 - (at & 0xFFF);
+        let mut n = page_left.min(((max - out.len()) * 2) as u32).min(0x200) & !1;
+        // a text may end close to the end of what is mapped: smaller pieces then
+        while n >= 2 && !g.readable(at, n) {
+            n = (n / 2) & !1;
+        }
+        if n < 2 {
+            return None;
+        }
+        let mut buf = vec![0u8; n as usize];
+        g.read(at, &mut buf);
+        for c in buf.chunks(2) {
+            let u = u16::from_le_bytes([c[0], c[1]]);
+            if u == 0 {
+                return Some(out);
+            }
+            out.push(u);
+        }
+        at = at.wrapping_add(n);
+    }
+    None
+}
+
+/// the text written into the ring with its end mark; its guest address. The same text placed
+/// not long ago (in the younger half of the ring, its bytes still the same) is handed out
+/// again instead.
+pub fn write_text(g: &mut dyn Guest, ring: &mut Ring, text: &[u16]) -> Option<u32> {
+    let mut bytes: Vec<u8> = text.iter().flat_map(|u| u.to_le_bytes()).collect();
+    bytes.extend_from_slice(&[0, 0]);
+    let (hash, len) = (fnv(&bytes), bytes.len() as u32);
+    let again: Vec<u32> = ring.young(hash, len).collect();
+    for at in again {
+        if g.readable(at, len) {
+            let mut have = vec![0u8; bytes.len()];
+            g.read(at, &mut have);
+            if have == bytes {
+                return Some(at);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    let (at, start) = ring.place_at(len)?;
+    if !g.readable(at, len) {
+        return None;
+    }
+    g.write(at, &bytes);
+    // texts this one wrote over are forgotten (the young check covers them too)
+    ring.recent.retain(|&(_, a, l, _)| a + l <= at || at + len <= a);
+    if ring.recent.len() >= RECENT {
+        ring.recent.remove(0);
+    }
+    ring.recent.push((hash, at, len, start));
+    Some(at)
+}
+
+/// the chapter the game is in, for `off` lines: the last chapter person file the game loaded
+/// (the Person archive list `Person::Load` appends to, `fates_rando::PERSON_LIST`), else
+/// GameUserData's current chapter. Read only when the file has `off` lines.
+pub fn chapter_now(g: &mut dyn Guest) -> Option<String> {
+    let mut last = None;
+    let mut node = g.r32(fates_rando::PERSON_LIST).unwrap_or(0);
+    for _ in 0..64 {
+        if node == 0 {
+            break;
+        }
+        if let Some(start) = g.r32(node).filter(|&s| s != 0) {
+            if let Some(name) = g.r32(start).filter(|&p| p != 0).and_then(|p| g.cstr(p, 32)) {
+                if let Some(k) = crate::fates_rando_chests::chapter_key(&String::from_utf8_lossy(&name)) {
+                    last = Some(k);
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        node = g.r32(node.wrapping_add(8)).unwrap_or(0);
+    }
+    last.or_else(|| crate::fates_rando_chests::chapter_key(&crate::fates_nuzlocke::chapter(g)))
+}
+
+/// the label at a trap place: `Mess::Get` keeps it in r7; `Mess::GetNoReplace` keeps none, so
+/// it is read from the `<label>_COM` both print into their stack buffer at sp (a label too
+/// long for the buffer is read as far as it goes).
+pub fn label_at(g: &mut dyn Guest, at: u32, r7: u32, sp: u32) -> Vec<u8> {
+    if at == MESS_GET_RETURN {
+        return g.cstr(r7, MAX_LABEL).unwrap_or_default();
+    }
+    let mut l = g.cstr(sp, LABEL_BUF).unwrap_or_default();
+    if l.ends_with(COM_SUFFIX) {
+        l.truncate(l.len() - COM_SUFFIX.len());
+    }
+    l
+}
+
+/// a built `Mess::Get` text: the message at `r4` starts with `$a` (arguments filled in).
+pub fn built(g: &mut dyn Guest, at: u32, r4: u32) -> bool {
+    if at != MESS_GET_RETURN || r4 == 0 || r4 % 2 != 0 || !g.readable(r4, 4) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    let mut b = [0u8; 4];
+    g.read(r4, &mut b);
+    b == [b'$', 0, b'a', 0]
+}
+
+/// what the trap does with one result: the text at `r0` (label `label`; `built` = arguments
+/// filled in by the game) passed through `swap`; Some(new r0) when it changed and was written
+/// into the ring.
+pub fn on_result(swap: &Swap, g: &mut dyn Guest, ring: &mut Ring, r0: u32, label: &[u8], built: bool,
+                 stats: &mut (u32, u32)) -> Option<u32>
+{
+    if built || label.starts_with(b"MPID_") {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let text = read_text(g, r0, MAX_TEXT_UNITS)?;
+    if text.is_empty() {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let changed = swap.apply(&text, stats)?;
+    if swap.has_off() && swap.off_in(chapter_now(g).as_deref()) {
+        return None;
+    }
+    write_text(g, ring, &changed)
+}
+
+// --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the trap
+
+/// the trap's state, kept with the randomizer session (`Session::names`).
+#[derive(Debug, Default)]
+pub struct Names {
+    /// the run whose `full.txt` was read last (None = no run)
+    run: Option<String>,
+    /// its pass, when it changes anything
+    swap: Option<Swap>,
+    /// the ring, once mapped
+    ring: Option<Ring>,
+    /// the words at TRAP_SITES the recompiled code was last checked against
+    lib_words: Option<[u32; 2]>,
+    /// said once: the word is a stranger's, the code cannot tell a changed function, or the
+    /// region is taken
+    noted: bool,
+    logged: HashSet<Vec<u8>>,
+    /// texts changed since the program started
+    pub changed: u64,
+}
+
+impl Names {
+    /// whether a pass is active.
+    pub fn active(&self) -> bool {
+        self.swap.is_some()
+    }
+}
+
+/// what the frame writes at MESS_GET_RETURN (as the chest trap: a stranger's word is left).
+pub fn word_to_write(want: bool, now: u32) -> Option<u32> {
+    match (want, now) {
+        (true, ORIGINAL_WORD) => Some(TRAP_WORD),
+        (false, TRAP_WORD) => Some(ORIGINAL_WORD),
+        _ => None,
+    }
+}
+
+/// the run's game as `full.txt` names it.
+pub fn game_name(fates_rando_game: Option<&str>) -> &str {
+    fates_rando_game.unwrap_or("fates")
+}
+
+/// the pass of a run's `full.txt` for this game: Ok(None) = nothing to do (no file, or
+/// nothing for this language); Err = the file is refused (said in a notice).
+pub fn swap_for(full: Option<Result<Full, String>>, seed: Option<&str>, game: &str) -> Result<Option<Swap>, String> {
+    let full = match full {
+        None => return Ok(None),
+        Some(Err(e)) => return Err(e),
+        Some(Ok(f)) => f,
+    };
+    if seed.is_some_and(|s| s != full.seed) {
+        return Err(format!("its seed {} is not the run's", full.seed));
+    }
+    if full.game != game {
+        return Err(format!("it was made for {}, this game is {game}", full.game));
+    }
+    Ok(Swap::new(&full, TEXT_LANG))
+}
+
+/// the ring's block, mapped now if it is not (Base region pages, RW); None when the range
+/// holds something else or no pages are free.
+fn ensure_ring(system: &mut System) -> Option<Ring> {
+    use crate::memory::{MemoryRegion, MemoryState, Permission};
+    let mapped = system.memory.mapping_at(RING_VADDR).copied();
+    match mapped {
+        Some(m) if m.base == RING_VADDR && m.size >= RING_SIZE => {
+            return Some(system.rando.names.ring.take().unwrap_or(Ring::new(RING_VADDR, RING_SIZE)));
+        }
+        Some(_) => return None,
+        None => {}
+    }
+    if system.memory.mappings().any(|m| {
+        (m.base as u64) < RING_VADDR as u64 + RING_SIZE as u64 && (RING_VADDR as u64) < m.base as u64 + m.size as u64
+    }) {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let block = system.memory.phys.allocate(MemoryRegion::Base, RING_SIZE)?;
+    system.memory.zero_physical(block.addr, block.size);
+    system.memory.map(RING_VADDR, block.addr, block.size, Permission::RW, MemoryState::Private);
+    log::info!("fates_rando: names hook: ring of buffers mapped at 0x{RING_VADDR:08X} (0x{RING_SIZE:X} bytes, Base pages 0x{:08X})",
+               block.addr);
+    Some(Ring::new(RING_VADDR, RING_SIZE))
+}
+
+/// `kernel::svc::dispatch` for SVC_NUMBER: true when it was the trap at one of TRAP_SITES (the
+/// replaced `add sp, sp, #80` done, the text changed when the pass changes it); false for any
+/// other place (the svc is then an unknown one, as before).
+pub fn on_svc(system: &mut System) -> bool {
+    let at = system.cpu.regs[15].wrapping_sub(4);
+    if system.cpu.cpsr.thumb || !TRAP_SITES.contains(&at) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    let taken = system.rando.names.swap.take();
+    if let Some(swap) = taken.as_ref() {
+        if system.rando.names.ring.is_none() || system.memory.mapping_at(RING_VADDR).is_none() {
+            system.rando.names.ring = ensure_ring(system);
+        }
+        if let Some(mut ring) = system.rando.names.ring.take() {
+            let r = &system.cpu.regs;
+            let (r0, r4, r7, sp) = (r[0], r[4], r[7], r[13]);
+            let label = label_at(&mut system.memory, at, r7, sp);
+            let built = built(&mut system.memory, at, r4);
+            let mut stats = (0u32, 0u32);
+            if let Some(new_r0) = on_result(swap, &mut system.memory, &mut ring, r0, &label, built, &mut stats) {
+                system.cpu.regs[0] = new_r0;
+                let n = &mut system.rando.names;
+                n.changed += 1;
+                if n.logged.len() < MAX_LOGGED && n.logged.insert(label.clone()) {
+                    log::info!("fates_rando: names hook: {} changed at 0x{at:08X} ({} names, {} voice commands dropped)",
+                               String::from_utf8_lossy(&label), stats.0, stats.1);
+                }
+            }
+            system.rando.names.ring = Some(ring);
+        }
+    }
+    system.rando.names.swap = taken;
+    system.cpu.regs[13] = system.cpu.regs[13].wrapping_add(STACK_ADD);
+    true
+}
+
+/// once a frame (`fates_rando_session::after_frame`, Fire Emblem Fates only): the pass follows
+/// the active run's `full.txt`, and the word at MESS_GET_RETURN follows the pass.
+pub fn frame(system: &mut System, data: &Path) {
+    let active = system.rando.active();
+    if system.rando.names.run != active {
+        let mut swap = None;
+        if let Some(id) = active.as_deref() {
+            let full = read_full(&crate::fates_rando_session::run_dir(data, id));
+            let seed = system.rando.active_seed();
+            let game = game_name(system.config.fates_rando_game.as_deref()).to_string();
+            match swap_for(full, seed.as_deref(), &game) {
+                Ok(Some(s)) => {
+                    log::info!("fates_rando: {id}: names in dialogue on ({} names, {} voices silenced)", s.names.len(),
+                               s.voices.len());
+                    swap = Some(s);
+                }
+                Ok(None) => {}
+                Err(e) => {
+                    log::warn!("fates_rando: {id}: dialogue names kept ({FILE}: {e})");
+                    system.rando.notices.push(format!("dialogue names kept ({FILE}: {e})"));
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        let n = &mut system.rando.names;
+        n.run = active;
+        n.swap = swap;
+    }
+    let mut want = system.rando.names.swap.is_some();
+    if want && system.recompiled.as_ref().is_some_and(|lib| !lib.checks_itself()) {
+        if !system.rando.names.noted {
+            system.rando.names.noted = true;
+            log::warn!("fates_rando: names in dialogue need the game recompiled again (its code cannot tell a changed \
+                        function); dialogue keeps the old names");
+        }
+        want = false;
+    }
+    if want && system.rando.names.ring.is_none() {
+        system.rando.names.ring = ensure_ring(system);
+        if system.rando.names.ring.is_none() {
+            if !system.rando.names.noted {
+                system.rando.names.noted = true;
+                let text = format!("dialogue names kept (guest memory at 0x{RING_VADDR:08X} is taken or none is free)");
+                log::warn!("fates_rando: {text}");
+                system.rando.notices.push(text);
+            }
+            want = false;
+        }
+    }
+    let mut words = [0u32; 2];
+    for (k, &site) in TRAP_SITES.iter().enumerate() {
+        let Some(now) = Guest::r32(&mut system.memory, site) else { return };
+        match word_to_write(want, now) {
+            Some(word) => {
+                system.memory.write32_privileged(site, word);
+                log::info!("fates_rando: names hook at 0x{site:08X} {}",
+                           if word == TRAP_WORD { "on" } else { "off (the original word back)" });
+            }
+            None if want && now != TRAP_WORD && !system.rando.names.noted => {
+                system.rando.names.noted = true;
+                log::warn!("fates_rando: the message word at 0x{site:08X} is 0x{now:08X}, not the original's; \
+                            the names hook stays off there");
+            }
+            None => {}
+        }
+        let Some(now) = Guest::r32(&mut system.memory, site) else { return };
+        words[k] = now;
+    }
+    if words != system.rando.names.lib_words.unwrap_or([ORIGINAL_WORD; 2]) {
+        if let Some(lib) = system.recompiled.as_mut() {
+            if lib.checks_itself() {
+                let stale = lib.check(&mut system.memory);
+                log::info!("fates_rando: names hook: the recompiled code checked again, {stale} functions run in the interpreter");
+            }
+        }
+        system.rando.names.lib_words = Some(words);
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod tests {
+    use super::*;
+    use std::collections::BTreeMap;
+
+    #[derive(Default)]
+    struct Fake {
+        blocks: BTreeMap<u32, Vec<u8>>,
+    }
+
+    impl Fake {
+        fn put(&mut self, at: u32, data: &[u8]) {
+            self.blocks.insert(at, data.to_vec());
+        }
+        fn find(&self, addr: u32, len: u32) -> Option<(u32, usize)> {
+            let (&b, v) = self.blocks.range(..=addr).next_back()?;
+            let o = (addr - b) as usize;
+            (o + len as usize <= v.len()).then_some((b, o))
+        }
+    }
+
+    impl Guest for Fake {
+        fn readable(&self, addr: u32, len: u32) -> bool {
+            self.find(addr, len).is_some()
+        }
+        fn read(&mut self, addr: u32, out: &mut [u8]) {
+            let (b, o) = self.find(addr, out.len() as u32).unwrap();
+            out.copy_from_slice(&self.blocks[&b][o..o + out.len()]);
+        }
+        fn write(&mut self, addr: u32, data: &[u8]) {
+            let (b, o) = self.find(addr, data.len() as u32).unwrap();
+            self.blocks.get_mut(&b).unwrap()[o..o + data.len()].copy_from_slice(data);
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn u(s: &str) -> Vec<u16> {
+        s.encode_utf16().collect()
+    }
+
+    fn hex16(s: &str) -> String {
+        u(s).iter().flat_map(|c| c.to_le_bytes()).map(|b| format!("{b:02x}")).collect()
+    }
+
+    fn hexa(s: &str) -> String {
+        s.bytes().map(|b| format!("{b:02x}")).collect()
+    }
+
+    /// a made-up file: Aaron -> Baker, Baker -> Carla, Carl -> Doran, voices AARON and BAKER
+    /// silenced (the names and keys are ours, no game text).
+    fn file() -> String {
+        format!("fates-rando-full 1\nseed demo-7\ngame fates\n\
+n E {} {}\nn E {} {}\nn E {} {}\ns {}\ns {}\nend 5\n",
+                hex16("Aaron"), hex16("Baker"), hex16("Baker"), hex16("Carla"), hex16("Carl"), hex16("Doran"),
+                hexa("AARON"), hexa("BAKER"))
+    }
+
+    fn swap() -> Swap {
+        Swap::new(&Full::parse(&file()).unwrap(), TEXT_LANG).unwrap()
+    }
+
+    fn run(s: &Swap, text: &str) -> Option<String> {
+        let mut st = (0, 0);
+        s.apply(&u(text), &mut st).map(|v| String::from_utf16(&v).unwrap())
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn the_file_is_read_whole_or_refused() {
+        let f = Full::parse(&file()).unwrap();
+        assert_eq!((f.seed.as_str(), f.game.as_str(), f.names.len(), f.silence.len()), ("demo-7", "fates", 3, 2));
+        assert_eq!(f.names[0], Name { lang: "E".into(), old: u("Aaron"), new: u("Baker") });
+        assert_eq!(f.silence[0], b"AARON".to_vec());
+        let t = file();
+        assert!(Full::parse(&t.replace("end 5\n", "")).is_err(), "cut short");
+        assert!(Full::parse(&t.replace("end 5", "end 4")).is_err());
+        assert!(Full::parse(&t.replace("full 1", "full 2")).is_err());
+        assert!(Full::parse(&format!("{t}s 41\n")).is_err(), "a line after end");
+        assert!(Full::parse(&t.replace(&hex16("Baker"), "4100610")).is_err(), "odd hex");
+        assert!(Full::parse(&t.replace("game fates\n", "")).is_err());
+        // an old name twice, or a pair that changes nothing
+        let twice = t.replace(&format!("n E {} {}", hex16("Carl"), hex16("Doran")),
+                              &format!("n E {} {}", hex16("Aaron"), hex16("Doran")));
+        assert!(Full::parse(&twice).is_err());
+        let same = t.replace(&format!("{} {}", hex16("Carl"), hex16("Doran")), &format!("{} {}", hex16("Carl"), hex16("Carl")));
+        assert!(Full::parse(&same).is_err());
+        // off lines; an empty file of this format is a file (the trap then stays off)
+        let off = Full::parse(&t.replace("end 5", "off C008\nend 6")).unwrap();
